WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.698
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.698 --> 00:00:10.837
If you grew up in Mexico you grew up going
to bakeries every day, the smells and

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:15.610
the flavors, just the experience to
pick the bundles and to pick the bread.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.240
My mom didn't like that I
grabbed the tray and the tongs,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.070
because I used to pile the tray up high.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.560
That's something that we wanted
to replicate here in the US.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.860
Ricardo and I met in business school.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.720
We actually were from
the same city in Mexico.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.840
We hadn't met until we
both were at Stanford.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.010
When we moved to LA,

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.230
we saw that that tradition was there
amongst the Latino communities.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:42.280
But there wasn't necessarily a place that
reflected exactly what it was like to

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.730
be home.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.390
That's the beginning of this idea.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.010
&gt;&gt; We are in our store in Highland Park
northeast of downtown Los Angeles,

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:56.000
very dense Hispanic community,
working class neighborhood.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.200
There is a lot of activity from
very early on in the morning,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.280
you see people coming and going.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.020
&gt;&gt; We've always tried to hire locally and

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.140
we make sure our products
are made from scratch.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.220
&gt;&gt; My favorite items on the menu, the
concha which is a traditional sweet bread.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.666
We have breakfast tacos in the morning.

00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:19.550
&gt;&gt; We make an ocha latte which,
that's pretty good over ice.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.340
&gt;&gt; When we first opened, we learned a lot
by just being at the first location.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.460
Pretty much all the time,
we actually, we would sleep there.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.600
If you're thinking of
doing your own business,

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.960
there's never going to be a better time
to do it than right after you graduate.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.780
The opportunity cost of
just deciding to go for

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.180
it is the lowest it's ever going to be.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.050
&gt;&gt; Some of the classes that I remember
from the GSB, performance leadership,

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.580
the entrepreneurship
classes were terrific.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.900
&gt;&gt; They have a lot of lessons and
teachings about how to

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.760
run an organization and how really it
comes down to building effective teams.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.210
One of the gifts of going to
the GSB is the alumni community.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.400
&gt;&gt; Some of the advisers of
our company are GSB alumni.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.120
So most,
if not all of our success is due to them.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.190
To create company culture
is a daily things.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:14.430
It's with the way we act, not only with
our employees, but with our customers,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.780
with our suppliers.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.380
I derive the most pleasure from people.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.590
You really have to enjoy people
to be in the restaurant business.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.321
That gives me great joy.

00:02:24.321 --> 00:02:27.290
&gt;&gt; I really like going our stores and
just talking to our staff.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:28.430
It's actually a little dangerous,

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.928
because you can spend a whole
day just talking to our team.

00:02:31.928 --> 00:02:37.473
&gt;&gt; Nobody goes angry into a bakery, which
is another plus of being in this business.

00:02:37.473 --> 00:02:44.906
[MUSIC]

